{:edb/db-before-t 2369 :edb/db-after-t 2370 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535682 50 #inst "2026-10-09T05:02:21.787Z" 13194139535682 true] [17592186147293 1100 "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?\n\n- $-2x^2y''+6xy'-5y=0$\n\n- $xy''+2y'-3xy=0$\n\n- $y''-3x^2y=\\sqrt{x}$" 13194139535682 true] [17592186147293 1100 "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?\n\n1. $-2x^2y''+6xy'-5y=0$\n2. $xy''+2y'-3xy=0$\n3. $y''-3x^2y=\\sqrt{x}$" 13194139535682 false] [17592186147293 1145 "The second-order Cauchy-Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\nax^2y''+bxy'+cy=f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$, and $c$ are constants. With that in mind, let's inspect each of the given equations.\n\n- Equation I is a Cauchy-Euler equation with $a=-2$, $b=6$, $c=-5$, and $f(x)=0$.\n- Equation II is *not* a Cauchy-Euler equation because the $y''$ is not multiplied by an $x^2$. Instead, it is multiplied by an $x$.\n- Equation III is *not* a Cauchy-Euler equation because the $y''$ term is not multiplied by an $x^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535682 false] [17592186147293 1145 "The second-order Cauchy-Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\nax^2y''+bxy'+cy=f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants. With that in mind, let's inspect each of the given equations.\n\n- Equation I is a Cauchy-Euler equation with $a=-2,$ $b=6,$ $c=-5,$ and $f(x)=0.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Cauchy-Euler equation because the $y''$ is not multiplied by an $x^2.$ Instead, it is multiplied by an $x.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Cauchy-Euler equation because the $y''$ term is not multiplied by an $x^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535682 true] [17592186147294 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n3t^2 y''+8ty' -2y = 0, \\quad t\\gt 0.\n$$" 13194139535682 true] [17592186147294 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$3t^2 y''+8ty' -2y = 0, \\quad t\\gt 0$." 13194139535682 false] [17592186147294 1145 "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form\n\n$$\ny=t^\\lambda\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda t^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)t^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3t^{2} \\cdot λ(λ - 1)t^{λ - 2} + 8t \\cdot λt^{λ - 1} - 2t^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\n3λ(λ - 1)t^{λ} + 8λt^{λ} - 2t^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nt^{λ}[3λ(λ - 1) + 8λ - 2] &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3λ(λ - 1) + 8λ - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n3λ^{2} + 5λ - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 2)(3λ - 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(t) &= c_{1}t^{-2} + c_{2}t^{1/3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{c_{1}}{t^{2}} + c_{2}t^{1/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535682 false] [17592186147294 1145 "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=t^\\lambda.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda t^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)t^{\\lambda-2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+8t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}-2{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 3\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+8\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }-2{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[3\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+8\\lambda -2] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+8\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ 3{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(3\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = -2$ and $\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{3}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={c}_{1}{t}^{-2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{2}}+{c}_{2}{t}^{1/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535682 true] [17592186147295 1100 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' +2 xy' -6 y= 0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{c_1}{x^3}+ c_2x^{2},\n$$\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' +2 xy' -6 y= 0, \\quad y(1)= 0,  \\quad y'(1) = 5, \\quad x \\gt 0.\n$$" 13194139535682 true] [17592186147295 1100 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$x^2 y'' +2 xy' -6 y= 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y=\\dfrac{c_1}{x^3}+ c_2x^{2}$,\n\nfind the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$x^2 y'' +2 xy' -6 y= 0, \\quad y(1)= 0, \\quad y'(1) = 5, \\quad x \\gt 0$." 13194139535682 false] [17592186147295 1145 "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0$. Substituting $y(1)= 0$ into the general solution gives\n$0 = c_1+c_2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad c_2 = -c_1$.\n- Then, we apply the condition $y'(1) = 5$. Differentiating $y$, we get\n$y^{′}|=-3\\frac{c_{1}}{x^{4}} + 2c_{2}x$.\nSubstituting $y'(1)=5$ and $c_2=-c_1$ into the above gives\n$5|=-3c_{1} + 2c_{2}; 5|=-3c_{1} + 2(-c_{1}); 5|=-5c_{1}; c_{1}|=-1⇒c_{2} = 1$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=x^{2}-\\dfrac{1}{x^3}\n$$" 13194139535682 false] [17592186147295 1145 "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0.$ Substituting $y(1)= 0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0 = c_1+c_2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad  c_2 = -c_1.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition $y'(1) = 5.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-3\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{4}}+2{c}_{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y'(1)=5$ and $c_2=-c_1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =-3{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ 5 & =-3{c}_{1}+2(-{c}_{1}) \\\\ 5 & =-5{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =-1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=x^{2}-\\dfrac{1}{x^3}.\n$$" 13194139535682 true] [17592186147374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360908 13194139535682 true] [17592186147374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360915 13194139535682 true] [17592186147374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360943 13194139535682 true] [17592186147374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360950 13194139535682 true] [17592186147375 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360922 13194139535682 true] [17592186147375 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360929 13194139535682 true] [17592186147375 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360957 13194139535682 true] [17592186147375 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360964 13194139535682 true] [17592186147376 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360936 13194139535682 true] [17592186147376 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360971 13194139535682 true] [17592186147376 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360978 13194139535682 true] [17592186147376 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360985 13194139535682 true] [17592186147376 1020 #edb/ref 17592186360992 13194139535682 true] [17592186148133 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535682 true] [17592186148133 1145 "The second-order Cauchy–Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}y′′+bxy′+cy=f(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that has the above form is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+4y=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139535682 true] [17592186148137 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535682 true] [17592186148137 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-2y=0,\\,t>0.\n$$" 13194139535682 true] [17592186148137 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$t^{2}y^{″} - 2y = 0, t > 0$." 13194139535682 false] [17592186148137 1145 "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-2{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-2{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-2] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =2$ and $\\lambda =-1.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1}.\n$$" 13194139535682 true] [17592186360908 1017 #edb/ref 17592186360909 13194139535682 true] [17592186360908 1089 "q-64780" 13194139535682 true] [17592186360908 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535682 true] [17592186360908 1100 "Which of the following equations is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation?" 13194139535682 true] [17592186360908 1113 #uuid "0af108b7-6c19-599e-bfad-892954501ac7" 13194139535682 true] [17592186360908 1145 "The second-order Cauchy–Euler equation has the form\n\n$$\na{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+bt{y}^{′}+cy=f(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that has the above form is\n\n$$\n2{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+4t{y}^{′}-3y=0.\n$$" 13194139535682 true] [17592186360909 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360910 13194139535682 true] [17592186360909 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360911 13194139535682 true] [17592186360909 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360912 13194139535682 true] [17592186360909 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360913 13194139535682 true] [17592186360909 1000 #edb/ref 17592186360914 13194139535682 true] [17592186360909 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535682 true] [17592186360909 1041 "selection" 13194139535682 true] [17592186360909 1104 #edb/ref 17592186360912 13194139535682 true] [17592186360909 1110 #uuid "624c4e2b-8940-5ce4-b07f-6a0b35ccb4c3" 13194139535682 true] [17592186360910 1034 "3{y}^{″}+2t{y}^{′}-{t}^{2}y=0" 131941